INT. VENTRILOQUIST'S ATTIC - NIGHT

A low, slant-roofed room under the eaves. Dozens of wooden dummies sit in rows on rickety shelves, glass eyes gleaming. A steamer trunk lies open on the floor, lined with red velvet. Rain taps the skylight.

MAXIMILIAN ROOK (70s, bow tie askew, dressing gown, white gloves) kneels beside the trunk, tenderly arranging the limbs of a dummy: a small boy in a sailor suit with a painted smile.

A KNOCK at the attic hatch. A WOMAN climbs through (30s, trench coat, a recorder in her hand), a journalist, shaking rain from her collar.

JOURNALIST
Mr. Rook? I rang the bell.

MAXIMILIAN
The bell doesn't work.

JOURNALIST
I noticed. The door was open.

MAXIMILIAN
It always is.

She looks around, scanning the shelves.

JOURNALIST
Quite the audience.

MAXIMILIAN
They're my family.

JOURNALIST
All of them?

MAXIMILIAN
Forty-one. Forty-two, with Percy.

He pats the sailor-boy. Its head lolls, and the painted smile shifts in the lamplight.

JOURNALIST
Odelia Fairweather, from the Courier. May I?

She sets her recorder on the trunk, clicks it on.

ODELIA
Mr. Rook, you retired in 1974. People say you stopped because you could no longer tell where your voice ended and theirs began.

MAXIMILIAN
People say a great deal.

ODELIA
Is it true?

MAXIMILIAN
(gently)
Ask Percy.

ODELIA
I'd rather ask you.

MAXIMILIAN
But he's the one who knows.

He lifts Percy onto his knee. His hand slides inside the dummy's back. Its jaw clacks.

MAXIMILIAN
(a piping boy's voice, lips unmoving)
Hello, lady.

Odelia laughs, uneasy.

ODELIA
Very good.

MAXIMILIAN
(the boy's voice, lips unmoving)
Max is old. He forgets to eat.

MAXIMILIAN
(in his own voice, startled)
Percy.

MAXIMILIAN
(the boy's voice, lips unmoving)
He forgets to sleep. He forgets which of us is which.

MAXIMILIAN
That is enough.

MAXIMILIAN
(the boy's voice, lips unmoving)
Max, stop moving your lips.

Maximilian's hand withdraws. He stares at it. The dummy's jaw hangs open. Silence in the attic. Rain.

ODELIA
(whispering)
Mr. Rook?

MAXIMILIAN
I did not say that.

ODELIA
I know.

MAXIMILIAN
I was not saying it.

Odelia reaches for the recorder. Her hand trembles. The little red light blinks. From the shelves, one by one, the painted heads begin to turn, wood clicking against wood, until forty-one pairs of glass eyes rest upon the journalist.

MAXIMILIAN
(the boy's voice, lips unmoving)
Hello, lady. Do stay.

Maximilian lifts his gloved hands and lets the dummy slide back into the velvet. It settles with a soft sigh. He stands, gown trailing.

MAXIMILIAN
Miss Fairweather. You had better go.

ODELIA
Mr. Rook, I...

MAXIMILIAN
Go down the stairs. Do not look at the shelves. Do not answer if anyone calls your name.

She snatches the recorder. She crosses to the hatch, one step at a time. Behind her, in the dark, forty-two small voices begin, very quietly, to hum.

At the hatch she stops.

ODELIA
Will you be all right?

MAXIMILIAN
They've never hurt me.

ODELIA
That isn't what I asked.

He turns to the shelves. The old man's shoulders drop, and he smiles for the first time, the exact smile painted on Percy's face.

MAXIMILIAN
Goodnight, my dear.

She climbs down. The hatch closes on its own.
